WEBVTT

NOTE The First Overnight Shift. Captions from the approved narration script.

1
00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:04.524 line:85%
It's nine at night.

2
00:00:04.574 --> 00:00:07.180 line:85%
You're arriving for your first overnight shift,

3
00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:09.384 line:85%
and the parents are ready to go to bed.

4
00:00:09.434 --> 00:00:14.075 line:85%
Before you take over, you need a few details that will guide the rest of the night.

5
00:00:14.125 --> 00:00:18.371 line:85%
In this lesson, you'll practice the arrival conversation, the safety checks,

6
00:00:18.421 --> 00:00:19.727 line:85%
and the morning handoff.

7
00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:26.982 line:85%
Start by asking when and how the baby last fed, how much they took if measured,

8
00:00:27.032 --> 00:00:29.377 line:85%
and what the plan is for the next feed.

9
00:00:29.427 --> 00:00:33.553 line:85%
Ask how the parents want to handle direct nursing, bottles, or both,

10
00:00:33.603 --> 00:00:36.870 line:85%
and which instructions came from the baby's clinician.

11
00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.818 line:85%
Ask when to wake a parent and how to reach them.

12
00:00:39.868 --> 00:00:44.056 line:85%
Confirm the home's address and the pediatrician's after hours number.

13
00:00:44.106 --> 00:00:46.942 line:85%
Locate the sleep space, clean feeding supplies,

14
00:00:46.992 --> 00:00:49.460 line:85%
and diaper supplies before you need them.

15
00:00:49.510 --> 00:00:53.452 line:85%
If the baby has a clinician directed plan, ask where it's written

16
00:00:53.502 --> 00:00:58.427 line:85%
and clarify anything you can't confidently follow before accepting responsibility

17
00:00:58.477 --> 00:00:59.287 line:85%
for that task.

18
00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:04.333 line:85%
One parent thinks the baby last fed at 7:30.

19
00:01:04.383 --> 00:01:06.675 line:85%
The other thinks it was closer to 8:00.

20
00:01:06.725 --> 00:01:09.498 line:85%
What would you clarify before they head to bed?

21
00:01:12.548 --> 00:01:17.199 line:85%
Ask for their best estimate, then confirm what to do if the timing is uncertain.

22
00:01:17.249 --> 00:01:21.782 line:85%
You could say, “If we aren't sure about the last feed, when should I wake you,

23
00:01:21.832 --> 00:01:24.426 line:85%
and what's the plan if the baby cues sooner?”

24
00:01:24.476 --> 00:01:26.424 line:85%
Record an estimate as an estimate.

25
00:01:26.474 --> 00:01:29.714 line:85%
Read the plan back so everyone knows what you agreed to.

26
00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:36.922 line:85%
The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends putting babies on their backs for

27
00:01:36.972 --> 00:01:37.653 line:85%
every sleep.

28
00:01:37.703 --> 00:01:41.430 line:85%
Use a separate, safety approved crib, bassinet, portable crib,

29
00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:45.389 line:85%
or play yard with a firm, flat, level surface and a fitted sheet.

30
00:01:45.439 --> 00:01:48.801 line:85%
Keep pillows, loose blankets, toys, and positioners out.

31
00:01:48.851 --> 00:01:51.603 line:85%
If a baby falls asleep in a swing or car seat,

32
00:01:51.653 --> 00:01:56.232 line:85%
move them to an appropriate sleep surface on their back as soon as possible.

33
00:01:56.282 --> 00:02:01.227 line:85%
If you're getting drowsy while holding the baby, put them in the safe sleep space.

34
00:02:01.277 --> 00:02:04.273 line:85%
If the setup isn't safe, raise it with the parents

35
00:02:04.323 --> 00:02:07.197 line:85%
and agree on a safe alternative before using it.

36
00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:13.373 line:85%
Wash your hands before preparing or handling feeding supplies.

37
00:02:13.423 --> 00:02:16.843 line:85%
Use clean equipment and follow the family's feeding plan.

38
00:02:16.893 --> 00:02:21.529 line:85%
Know what's in the bottle and when it was prepared, warmed, or first offered.

39
00:02:21.579 --> 00:02:26.033 line:85%
CDC guidance says prepared infant formula should be used within 2 hours of

40
00:02:26.083 --> 00:02:29.137 line:85%
preparation and within 1 hour of starting the feed.

41
00:02:29.187 --> 00:02:33.823 line:85%
If you won't use freshly prepared formula right away, refrigerate it promptly

42
00:02:33.873 --> 00:02:35.467 line:85%
and use it within 24 hours.

43
00:02:35.517 --> 00:02:38.510 line:85%
Throw out formula left in the bottle after a feed.

44
00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:41.309 line:85%
Expressed breast milk follows different rules.

45
00:02:41.359 --> 00:02:45.326 line:85%
Once warmed or brought to room temperature, use it within 2 hours.

46
00:02:45.376 --> 00:02:50.195 line:85%
CDC also allows leftover breast milk to be used within 2 hours after the feeding

47
00:02:50.245 --> 00:02:50.500 line:85%
ends.

48
00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:54.395 line:85%
Respect any earlier storage limit, and if the timing is unclear,

49
00:02:54.445 --> 00:02:56.708 line:85%
check with the parent before using it.

50
00:02:56.758 --> 00:03:01.455 line:85%
Follow the baby's clinician's directions for any special feeding instructions.

51
00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:09.345 line:85%
If the baby stops breathing, is unresponsive, is struggling severely to breathe,

52
00:03:09.395 --> 00:03:13.054 line:85%
or has blue or gray lips or face, call 911 immediately

53
00:03:13.104 --> 00:03:15.871 line:85%
and follow the dispatcher's instructions.

54
00:03:15.921 --> 00:03:20.267 line:85%
Notify the parents as soon as you can without delaying the call.

55
00:03:20.317 --> 00:03:22.259 line:85%
Here's a different situation.

56
00:03:22.309 --> 00:03:26.380 line:85%
A six week old has a rectal temperature of 100.4°F, or 38°C.

57
00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:30.227 line:85%
Wake the parent immediately and contact the pediatrician

58
00:03:30.277 --> 00:03:34.073 line:85%
or after hours service right away for urgent evaluation.

59
00:03:34.123 --> 00:03:38.607 line:85%
Don't wait for the morning handoff or for the temperature to rise.

60
00:03:38.657 --> 00:03:44.033 line:85%
Record the time, what you observed, whom you contacted, and what happened next.

61
00:03:44.083 --> 00:03:47.193 line:85%
If a life threatening sign develops, call 911.

62
00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:53.873 line:85%
A useful note gives the next caregiver something they can act on.

63
00:03:53.923 --> 00:03:59.013 line:85%
Log the time, the feeding method and measured amount if known, diaper changes,

64
00:03:59.063 --> 00:04:01.386 line:85%
sleep, and any unusual observations.

65
00:04:01.436 --> 00:04:03.758 line:85%
Use the family's agreed private log.

66
00:04:03.808 --> 00:04:07.844 line:85%
For example: “11:05 p.m., formula, 2 oz offered, 1.5 oz taken.

67
00:04:07.894 --> 00:04:08.569 line:85%
Wet diaper.

68
00:04:08.619 --> 00:04:10.348 line:85%
Back in bassinet at 11:30.”

69
00:04:10.398 --> 00:04:13.050 line:85%
Those are observations for this scenario.

70
00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:15.422 line:85%
The amounts are not feeding targets.

71
00:04:15.472 --> 00:04:19.178 line:85%
In the morning, start with anything that needs attention.

72
00:04:19.228 --> 00:04:23.132 line:85%
Then summarize the last feed and diaper, how the baby slept,

73
00:04:23.182 --> 00:04:24.846 line:85%
and any agreed next steps.

74
00:04:24.896 --> 00:04:29.393 line:85%
If there was a concern overnight, include who you contacted and when.

75
00:04:29.443 --> 00:04:33.083 line:85%
Confirm that the parent has taken over before you leave.

76
00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:39.579 line:85%
Before you leave this lesson, open the First Overnight Shift worksheet.

77
00:04:39.629 --> 00:04:43.273 line:85%
Write one question you'd ask when the last feed time is unclear,

78
00:04:43.323 --> 00:04:46.217 line:85%
then practice recording the sample feed and diaper.

79
00:04:46.267 --> 00:04:50.316 line:85%
The worksheet also gives you a quick reference for safe sleep, feeding,

80
00:04:50.366 --> 00:04:51.470 line:85%
and urgent concerns.

81
00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:54.761 line:85%
When the lesson is complete, take the five question quiz.

82
00:04:54.811 --> 00:04:58.051 line:85%
Four correct answers earn your Certificate of Completion.
